```javascript
// async_function.js

// Function to perform a task
function task(taskId) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    setTimeout(() => {
      if (Math.random() > 0.5) {
        // Simulate a task that takes a random amount of time to complete
        setTimeout(() => {
          resolve(`Task ${taskId} completed successfully`);
        }, Math.floor(Math.random() * 1000));
      } else {
        reject(`Task ${taskId} failed with error`);
      }
    }, Math.floor(Math.random() * 1000));
  });
}

// async_function
async function async_function(tasks) {
  // Use async/await to perform concurrent tasks
 const results = await Promise.all(tasks.map(taskId => task(taskId)));

  // Return the results of the tasks
  return results.flat();
}

// Example usage
const tasks = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
console.log(await async_function(tasks));
```

In this example, the `task` function simulates a task that takes a random amount of time to complete. The `async_function` function uses `Promise.all` to perform all the tasks concurrently and then flattens the results into a single array. The `await` keyword is used to wait for all the tasks to complete before returning the results.

You can modify the `task` function to perform any type of task that requires a random amount of time to complete, such as making API calls or reading files. The `async_function` function should work similarly, allowing you to perform concurrent tasks using async/await in JavaScript.
